sections.body:
Writing a Data Access Object by hand -- a class with methods like `findById`, `save`, `deleteById`, each hand-coding a JDBC or `EntityManager` call -- is exactly the kind of repetitive plumbing Spring Data JPA exists to remove. You declare an interface:

```java
public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {
}
```

...and that's it. `JpaRepository<Order, Long>` (parameterized by the entity type and its id type) extends `PagingAndSortingRepository`, which extends `CrudRepository`, and together this hierarchy already declares `save`, `findById`, `findAll`, `existsById`, `deleteById`, `count`, plus JPA-specific extras like `flush()` and batch-oriented `saveAll`/`deleteAllInBatch`. Spring Data JPA does not require you to implement a single one of these methods. At application startup, it generates an actual, working implementation of the interface -- a runtime proxy class -- and registers *that* as the bean satisfying `OrderRepository` wherever it's injected. This is worth stating plainly because it's easy to assume 'there must be a class somewhere with this logic': there genuinely isn't one you wrote, and largely isn't one you'll ever need to look at -- the interface itself, plus Spring Data's understanding of the `Order` entity's mapped properties, is enough information to generate a correct implementation.

Beyond the inherited CRUD methods, you can declare additional methods whose *name* Spring Data parses into a query, with no method body and no annotation required for the common cases:

```java
public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {
    List<Order> findByCustomerEmail(String email);
    List<Order> findByStatusAndCustomerEmail(OrderStatus status, String email);
    List<Order> findByTotalGreaterThanOrderByTotalDesc(BigDecimal minTotal);
    boolean existsByCustomerEmail(String email);
    long countByStatus(OrderStatus status);
}
```

The naming convention follows a fixed grammar: a subject (`find`/`exists`/`count`/`delete`), an optional `By`, then one or more property expressions joined with `And`/`Or`, each optionally suffixed with a comparison keyword (`GreaterThan`, `LessThanEqual`, `Like`, `IsNull`, `Between`, and others), and an optional `OrderBy<Property><Asc|Desc>` clause. Property names in the method must correspond to actual fields (or nested paths, using camelCase concatenation, like `findByCustomerAddressCity` navigating `customer.address.city`) on the entity's mapped model -- not database column names.

Crucially, this parsing happens once, when the `ApplicationContext` starts and Spring Data builds the repository proxy -- it inspects every declared method on the interface against the entity's metamodel at that point, not the first time each method is actually called. If a method name references a property that doesn't exist -- `findByCustomerName` on an `Order` entity that has no `customerName` field, only `customerEmail` -- the application fails to start at all, with a `PropertyReferenceException` naming exactly the unresolvable property and the entity type it looked for it on. This is the same fail-fast philosophy you've already seen with unsatisfiable dependency injection and ambiguous `@RequestMapping`s: a structural mistake is caught immediately, at startup, rather than deferred until the one request that happens to call that specific method.

Derived query methods get unwieldy fast once a query needs anything beyond a handful of ANDed conditions -- there's a real point past which `findByStatusAndCustomerEmailAndCreatedAtAfterOrderByCreatedAtDesc` is worse, not better, than writing the query out directly. `@Query` lets you supply **JPQL** (Jakarta Persistence Query Language) explicitly:

```java
public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {

    @Query("select o from Order o join o.customer c where c.email = :email and o.status = :status")
    List<Order> findActiveOrdersForCustomer(@Param("email") String email, @Param("status") OrderStatus status);
}
```

The critical thing to internalize about JPQL is that it queries the **entity model**, not the database schema directly: `from Order o` refers to the `Order` *entity* (its Java class name, by default, or an explicit `@Entity(name = ...)` if given one), `o.customer` navigates a mapped association by its *field name*, and `c.email` refers to a mapped *property*, not necessarily a literally-named database column. Two consequences follow from this. First, JPQL is somewhat database-portable -- the same JPQL can run against PostgreSQL or MySQL, because Hibernate translates it into the appropriate dialect-specific SQL underneath, whatever the target database happens to be. Second, and just as important for debugging: if you rename a database column via `@Column(name = ...)` without changing the Java field name, your JPQL doesn't need to change at all, because it was never referring to the column name in the first place -- only native queries, covered next, care about the actual column.

