A bidirectional one-to-many relationship -- an `Order` that has many `OrderItem`s, and each `OrderItem` that knows which `Order` it belongs to -- needs exactly one foreign-key column in the database (`order_id` on the `order_items` table), even though the object model has references pointing in both directions. JPA needs to know which side of the Java relationship is responsible for that column:

```java
@Entity
public class Order {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "order", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
    private List<OrderItem> items = new ArrayList<>();
}

@Entity
public class OrderItem {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;

    @ManyToOne
    @JoinColumn(name = "order_id")
    private Order order;

    private String productName;
}
```

`OrderItem.order`, annotated `@ManyToOne` with `@JoinColumn(name = "order_id")`, is the **owning side** -- it is the side that actually corresponds to the foreign-key column, and changes to it are what Hibernate looks at when deciding what to write to that column. `Order.items`, annotated `@OneToMany(mappedBy = "order")`, is the **inverse side** -- `mappedBy = "order"` explicitly says 'this collection is not itself backed by a column; it mirrors the `order` field declared over on `OrderItem`.' Get this backward, or omit `mappedBy` entirely on the `@OneToMany` side, and Hibernate assumes it's looking at *two separate, independent* relationships rather than one relationship viewed from two sides -- commonly manifesting as an unwanted extra join table being created, or updates made through one side of the relationship simply not being reflected through the other, because as far as Hibernate is concerned, they were never actually the same relationship in the first place.

The fix is to tell Hibernate, *at the specific query that needs it*, to load the association in the same round trip via a genuine SQL join, rather than leaving it lazy and letting it trigger extra queries later. In JPQL, that's `JOIN FETCH`:

```java
@Query("select distinct o from Order o join fetch o.items where o.status = :status")
List<Order> findWithItemsByStatus(@Param("status") OrderStatus status);
```

This produces one SQL query with an actual `JOIN` against `order_items`, populating every returned order's `items` collection immediately -- looping over the result afterward and calling `.getItems()` triggers zero additional queries, because the data is already there. (`distinct` matters here for a reason worth knowing: joining a one-to-many association naturally produces one result row per matched child, so without `distinct`, an order with 3 items would appear 3 times in the JPQL result before Hibernate's result-mapping de-duplicates entity instances -- `distinct` at the JPQL level tells Hibernate to filter the duplicate rows before that point.)

`@EntityGraph` offers a declarative alternative that works alongside a derived query method, where you can't easily insert a JOIN FETCH clause into a method name:

```java
@EntityGraph(attributePaths = "items")
List<Order> findByStatus(OrderStatus status);
```

Both approaches solve the same problem the same way -- collapsing what would have been N+1 round trips into one -- but they apply it precisely to the query that declares it, rather than changing the association's default fetch behavior everywhere. One caution worth flagging explicitly: `JOIN FETCH`-ing more than one `@OneToMany` collection on the same entity in a single query (an order's items *and* its separately-collectioned discount codes, say) multiplies rows together in the underlying SQL join -- a genuine Cartesian product -- which can produce a surprisingly large, slow result set instead of the fix you were going for; fetching multiple collections safely generally means separate queries (or `@EntityGraph`'s subgraph support) rather than one query joining everything at once.

examples.code:
@Entity
public class Order {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "order", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
    private List<OrderItem> items = new ArrayList<>();

    public void addItem(OrderItem item) {
        items.add(item);
        item.setOrder(this);   // keep both sides of the in-memory relationship consistent
    }
}

@Entity
public class OrderItem {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;

    @ManyToOne
    @JoinColumn(name = "order_id")
    private Order order;

    private String productName;
}

@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public void printOrderSizes() {
    List<Order> orders = orderRepository.findAll();       // query 1

    for (Order order : orders) {
        System.out.println(order.getItems().size());       // one query PER order
    }
}
// With 3 orders: 4 total queries -- barely noticeable.
// With 50,000 orders: 50,001 total queries -- a production incident.

public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {

    @Query("select distinct o from Order o join fetch o.items where o.status = :status")
    List<Order> findWithItemsByStatus(@Param("status") OrderStatus status);
}

@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public void printOrderSizes(OrderStatus status) {
    List<Order> orders = orderRepository.findWithItemsByStatus(status);   // query 1, with a join

    for (Order order : orders) {
        System.out.println(order.getItems().size());   // zero additional queries -- already loaded
    }
}

